WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.748
Tools are the bridge between model output
and real systems.

2
00:00:05.828 --> 00:00:11.075
A read-only tool may retrieve a status or
lookup a document.

3
00:00:11.155 --> 00:00:17.452
A write-capable tool may modify a file,
send a message, create a ticket,

4
00:00:17.532 --> 00:00:21.835
update a repository, or change a cloud
resource.

5
00:00:21.915 --> 00:00:27.897
Because tools can connect agent decisions
to real data and actions,

6
00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:31.546
tool-call logs are security-critical.

7
00:00:31.626 --> 00:00:38.343
They show where the agent moved from
conversation into operational effect.

8
00:00:38.423 --> 00:00:44.090
A high-impact action audit trail should
include the requester,

9
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.633
the model-proposed action, tool name,

10
00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:54.430
tool arguments or a safe summary,
authorization decision, policy version,

11
00:00:54.510 --> 00:01:01.962
approval status, executed action,
downstream result, and any rollback reference.

12
00:01:02.042 --> 00:01:04.770
If a human approved the action,

13
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:10.413
the audit trail should show what they saw
when they approved it.

14
00:01:10.493 --> 00:01:16.055
Approval that cannot be reconstructed
later is weak evidence.

15
00:01:16.135 --> 00:01:22.432
Tool monitoring should also record
validation and control decisions.

16
00:01:22.512 --> 00:01:28.914
Argument validation, allowlists, rate
limits, transaction previews,

17
00:01:28.994 --> 00:01:34.557
dry-run modes, approval gates, and policy
denials all matter.

18
00:01:34.637 --> 00:01:41.878
A denied action can be as important as an
executed one because repeated denials may

19
00:01:41.958 --> 00:01:49.410
show user confusion, prompt manipulation,
attempted misuse, or a broken workflow.

20
00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:56.207
Logs should capture decisions without
relying only on the final model text.

21
00:01:56.287 --> 00:02:01.010
Tool logs must be tied to identity and
authorization.

22
00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:06.862
The system should distinguish the user who
requested the action,

23
00:02:06.942 --> 00:02:14.183
the application identity that brokered it,
the model or prompt version involved,

24
00:02:14.263 --> 00:02:19.721
and the service identity used against the
downstream system.

25
00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:27.357
This makes investigation possible and
helps prevent models from becoming invisible

26
00:02:27.437 --> 00:02:32.580
middle layers between people and
privileged operations.
